    "markdown": "---\ntitle: \"But sometimes it's too cold for a heat pump\"\ndate: 2026-07-17\n# categories: []\n---\n\nHeat pumps are the most efficient way to heat a Canadian home — until the mercury really drops. Every cold-climate heat pump has a **minimum operating temperature**, the point below which the compressor can no longer pull enough heat out of the outdoor air and a backup heat source has to take over. Critics point to this as a fatal flaw for a country that regularly sees -30°C. Supporters point out that those hours are rarer than people think, and getting rarer to matter as cold-climate models push their limits colder every year.\n\nWho's right depends entirely on *where you live*. So instead of arguing in the abstract, let's look at six years of real hourly temperature records (2020–2025) from 300+ Environment Canada stations and let your own location answer the question.\n\nYou can use this map to find your nearest weather station (supports pan and zoom).\n\n::: {layout=\"[[60,40]]\"}\n\n::: {#8ee90160 .cell execution_count=1}\n\n::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display}\n```{=html}\n<div style=\"height:525px; width:100%;\">            <script src=\"https://cdnjs.cloudflare.com/ajax/libs/mathjax/2.7.5/MathJax.js?config=TeX-AMS-MML_SVG\"></script><script>if (window.MathJax && window.MathJax.Hub && window.MathJax.Hub.Config) {window.MathJax.Hub.Config({SVG: {font: \"STIX-Web\"}});}</script>                <script>window.PlotlyConfig = {MathJaxConfig: 'local'};</script>\n        <script charset=\"utf-8\" src=\"https://cdn.plot.ly/plotly-3.7.0.min.js\" integrity=\"sha256-jvTGqxNp8AGWEcvNLVuKr+8j5dGe9Yw51LQkmDH+IYA=\" crossorigin=\"anonymous\"></script>                <div id=\"f40e97b2-47e7-42b1-963e-d8ad45197695\" class=\"plotly-graph-div\" style=\"height:100%; width:100%;\"></div>            <script>                window.PLOTLYENV=window.PLOTLYENV || {};                                if (document.getElementById(\"f40e97b2-47e7-42b1-963e-d8ad45197695